From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Gazelka <andrew@ix.dev>
Date: Sun, 19 Jul 2026 01:30:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] build: add a typed per-derivation no-progress deadline

One wedged derivation -- a compiler gone silent, a builder stuck in
D-state -- otherwise stalls a nix-fast-build run until the whole-job
timeout, because there is no per-derivation liveness policy. Add an
opt-in --max-no-progress-seconds (with a --max-no-progress-seconds-
big-parallel escalation for legitimately long-quiet builds): a
BuildLivenessMonitor subscribes to the Nix daemon's build-status
directory, counts CPU, I/O, and build-log movement across the builder
process tree as progress, and fails a derivation whose deadline passes
with none, instead of pinning the run. The policy is typed
(LivenessPolicy), threaded to Nix through nix_options, and also bounds
clients that never acquire a daemon-owned goal.

Depends on a daemon that maintains the build-status directory and
enforces the matching setting (index's nix fork, patches 0003-0009 and
0021), so this is CI-runner policy, not a standalone upstream feature.
Ships tests (tests/test_liveness.py) run by the package checkPhase.

Refs indexable-inc/index#3317
Refs indexable-inc/index#3638

